# AuraConsumerApp rules

Rules for this repo only, learned from incidents. The topic rule files (`architecture.md`, `logging.md` and the rest) cover general practice.

- **`AudioSessionManager` can silently ignore an audio-session mode change.** `areCompatible` treats any two `.playAndRecord` configurations as compatible, whatever their modes, and the mode priority ranks `.videoChat` above `.voiceChat`. So once a `.playAndRecord` session is active, a request for a different mode logs "No reconfiguration needed" and keeps the current mode. Any change or A/B of the voice session's mode must make the new mode actually apply, and must check the session's real mode on device, not the requested one. Why: the `.voiceChat` vs `.videoChat` A/B had no effect until #1904 (still open) made a different mode from the same caller reapply (ENGMOB-3014).
- **The profile `locale` is sent only at sign-up, as `"en"`.** `POST /users` (`CreateUserRequest`) carries it; `PATCH /users` (`UpdateUserProfileRequest`) has no `locale` field, so an Aura member who starts Aurie keeps their own locale. The user record is shared with Aura. Mapping the phone language at sign-up waits until Aurie is localized.
- **Never look a profile up by email.** A Password sign-up tries to create the Auth and handles Firebase's "already exists" error; a Google or Apple sign-in fetches or creates the profile by the Auth's uid. Do not add a call to `/users/email/{email}/exists` or anything like it. Why: the backend is removing email lookups (ENGBACK-2463).
- **Sim tests usually run on a prod build against prod services, with the test accounts in `AuraConsumerAppSimTests/users.json`.** That is the normal setup, not an exception. Voice in particular only works there: `services.dev` mints production LiveKit tokens, so don't sim-test voice against dev.
- **The weekly screens PR (`screens-weekly/*`) is generated.** The `aurie-screens-weekly` routine opens it every Monday. Don't hand-edit its branch; fix the recipe or the routine, and the next run picks the fix up.
- **App Store releases are cut by the engineers.** Agents use `release-feature-branch-ios` for QA builds and do not run `release-ios` unless asked to.
- **Every link source goes through `DeepLinkHandler.handleCentralRouting`, and only there.** A new source (a push type, an SDK callback, a URL scheme) parses its input into routing data and calls it; it never navigates, never waits for the app to load, and never checks sign-in itself. The handler checks the ids, then holds the link until the app has loaded and, for a route that needs an account, until the user is signed in **and** has finished AI onboarding; logout drops it. Log and report a link by its route name (`DeepLinkRoute.name`) or host, never the URL or the payload, and send a link's labels only through `DeepLinkSafety.safeLabel`. Why: each listener used to wait 10 s on its own and then route anyway, Branch didn't wait at all, and a Firebase user counted as "signed in", so an existing Aura account tapping a link mid-onboarding was sent to Home.
- **`project.yml` holds no comments, and every target carries the app's version.** The `updateVersion` lane rewrites the file through YAML on every release, which drops comments, so the reason for an entry goes in a rule file instead. The same lane sets `MARKETING_VERSION` and `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION` on every target, and `release-ios` refuses to start when the targets disagree on main. So a new target starts at the app's current version and is added to `updateVersion` and to `VERSION_TARGETS` in the release script. Why: the `AurieWidget` target merged at 1.33.15 (6) from its branch base while the app was at 1.33.17 (1), and the 1.34.0 release could not start.
- **The app target also compiles the widget's views and assets** (`AurieWidget/Assets.xcassets`, `AurieWidgetEntry.swift`, `AurieWidget/Views`), so the in-app asks show the real widget, not a second drawing of it that drifts. The extension's entry points (bundle, configuration and provider) stay out of the app target.
